WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:03.420
The editors of Science Robotics have chosen

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.080
the most exciting robotic technologies for 2018.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.340
Here are just some of the big innovations from last year

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.740
A new video of the world's most dynamic humanoid

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.360
show’s Boston Dynamics’ Atlas jumping

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.620
over a log with one leg, and bounding up increasingly

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.060
higher wooden boxes without breaking pace.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.860
These feats add to walking on challenging terrain,

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.960
lifting and manipulating objects,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.500
and executing a backflip like a gymnast.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.760
For this latest parkour-type behavior,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.680
the control software uses the arms, legs, and

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:40.900
torso to manage energy.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.640
Atlas also has a novel visual sensing system

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.700
to judge each leap.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.460
Although these behaviors are not achieved for all trials,

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.980
the demonstrations serve as an inspiration

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.720
for what robots can do in the near future.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.540
This tailless flapping robot is a perfect example

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.720
of bioinspired design—

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.520
design that helps both to develop new technologies

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:06.060
and to unveil the secrets of how

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:08.320
nature builds living things.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.120
Surprisingly, this bot can accurately reproduce

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.840
the rapid escape maneuvers of fruit flies

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.360
even with no explicit control of all its rotational axes.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.600
And although it is over 50 times the size of a fruit fly

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.660
and does not mimic the wing morphology

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.700
or kinematics of any specific natural flyer,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.320
it shows that the bot can serve as a physical model

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.950
to test how flying organisms control flight.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.500
The editors consider it a paradigmatic example

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.520
of “science for robotics and robotics for science”

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.360
and expect that it will advance the development

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.360
of all flying robots.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.120
This year we saw a self-assembling DNA origami

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.100
structure that can be moved precisely by externally

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.100
applied electric fields.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.120
That means nanoscale DNA robots

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.160
can be controlled by a macro-level switch.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.240
Molecular robots like these can be used for

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:02.620
transport of molecules or nanoparticles over

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.120
tens of nanometers and open the door to

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.860
programmable synthesis and assembly of materials

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.220
from the bottom up.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.660
If you need an exoskeleton to help you move,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.320
you probably don’t want it to be as heavy

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.060
as Iron Man’s.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.220
A new light-weight, stretchy exosuit

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.720
offers new ways of integrating fabric design, sensing,

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:26.740
robotic control, and actuation to increase the wearer’s

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.300
strength, balance and endurance.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.420
And human-in-the-loop control allows better

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.080
integration with natural movement.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.340
The return of Aibo,

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.040
first introduced nearly 20 years ago by Sony,

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.980
brings increasing awareness of the role

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.660
social robots can play in childhood learning

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:46.800
or as companions for the aged.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.960
Understanding the perceptions, interactions,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.660
and expectations of the people around the robot,

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:54.060
and developing robot behaviors and personalities

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.760
that are context aware

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.100
are key challenges of social robotics.

